X:1
T:Peth Mawr ydy Cariad
T:Love is a Great Force
R:cân
S:https://amgueddfa.cymru/casgliadau/caneuongwerin/?id=4 (collected by D. Roy Saer)
N:Sung by Miss M. M. Williams (born 1894), a teacher, of Idanfryn, Brynsiencyn, Anglesey; recorded 30.4.59 (St Fagans tape 171). She learnt it as a small child from her father, a Brynsiencyn man, who she believed had picked it up in the "llofft stabal" (the farm servants' loft over the stable) when he was a farm servant. A young man's parents oppose his love, so he spends seven years on a man-of-war; on the very evening he comes home he goes to see his sweetheart again. Her father never sang her the fifth verse, thinking it improper: it revealed that a child as well as a sweetheart was waiting for the exile. The nonsense refrain is typical of songs recorded in Anglesey. Compare the tune of "Dydd Llun y Bore" / "Cerdd y Gog Lwydlas" in the Journal of the Welsh Folk Song Society, ii, 274.
N:Small notes on the score split the crotchet D on "mawr" and "gyr-" and the crotchet G on "llef-" into two quavers, for verses with an extra syllable. In verses 2, 3 and 4 the start of bar 2 ("el-o fo'n", G c2 B) is sung G G c B. In verse 3 the end of bar 7 and the start of bar 8 (A2 G F | G A) are sung A A A>G | F/F/ A. The A on "drwm" has a small + after its dot on the score, and the slur on "geir-iau" is dotted. Bar 9 is in 5/4, as in the source.
